## Course overview

Environmental Systems and Societies (ESS) is an **interdisciplinary** course that bridges the natural sciences and the humanities. Students study ecosystems and environmental problems while weighing the social, ethical, economic, and political dimensions of how societies respond.

Because it is transdisciplinary, ESS can satisfy **either Group 3 (Individuals & Societies) or Group 4 (Sciences)** in the diploma — giving students flexibility in their subject combination.

## What you will study

- **Foundations** — systems, sustainability, and environmental value systems.
- **Ecosystems and ecology** — energy, biodiversity, and population dynamics.
- **Biodiversity and conservation**.
- **Water, food, and soil systems**.
- **Atmosphere and climate change**.
- **Human populations and resource use**.

## Assessment

| Component | HL | SL | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Paper 1 | 35% | 40% | Case study and data analysis |
| Paper 2 | 40% | 35% | Short answers and structured essays |
| Internal Assessment | 25% | 25% | An individual environmental investigation |
